# Requests for the REST endpoints. Start the app and the stub
# (python samples/refdata-stub.py). See docs/running-locally.md. Run a request with the green arrow in the gutter.
# Change @host if the app is not on port 9090.
@host = http://localhost:9090

### A1 London entity, trade PO
# Expect one CSV row: CP_RepCp 549300CP1 plus zeros, CP_NatureOfRepCp C, CP_SecOfRepCp CDTI, CP_BranchOfRepCp GB (the LDNBB prefix is a London entity), CP_CpSide GIVE (PO is a GIVE code).
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A1",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP1",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### A2 non-London entity, trade PI
# Expect CP_BranchOfRepCp empty (BMOCM01 is not a London prefix) and CP_CpSide TAKE (PI is a TAKE code).
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A2",
  "trade_type": "PI",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "BMOCM01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP2",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### A3 LDN- prefix, trade RP
# LDN- is the second London prefix: CP_BranchOfRepCp GB. RP is a GIVE code: CP_CpSide GIVE.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A3",
  "trade_type": "RP",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDN-02",
  "counterpartyId": "CP3",
  "group_cd": "G2"
}

### A4 trade LP gives TAKE
# LP is in the TAKE list of the cpSide lookup: CP_CpSide TAKE.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A4",
  "trade_type": "LP",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP4",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### A5 unknown trade_type
# A code that is not in the lookup gives an empty CP_CpSide. The row is still written.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A5",
  "trade_type": "XX",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP5",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### A6 trade_type missing
# A missing field gives an empty CP_CpSide. The row is still written.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A6",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP6",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### A7 reference data has no nature
# The stub leaves out natureOfReportingCounterparty for counterpartyId NONATURE, so CP_NatureOfRepCp falls back to the default F.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "A7",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "NONATURE",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### B1 malformed JSON
# HTTP 200 with outcome FAILED and reason Malformed JSON. Nothing is written.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{not json

### B2 empty body
# HTTP 200 with outcome FAILED and reason Empty message.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json


### B3 JSON array, not an object
# HTTP 200 with outcome FAILED and reason Not a JSON object.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

[1, 2]

### C1 reference data answers 400
# A 4xx is never retried. HTTP 200 with outcome FAILED and reason Reference data HTTP 400. Needs the stub (samples/refdata-stub.py).
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "C1",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "FAIL4XX",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### C2 reference data answers 503
# A 5xx is retried (3 attempts, short backoff, under a second) and then fails: outcome FAILED, reason Reference data unavailable.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "C2",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "FAIL5XX",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### C3 reference data too slow
# The stub waits 8 seconds, longer than the 5 second read timeout. Three attempts take about 15 seconds, then outcome FAILED, reason Reference data unavailable. Be patient.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "C3",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "SLOW",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### D1 first row, flush=false
# The row waits in the batch: no report file yet.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message?flush=false
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "D1",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP1",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### D2 second row, flush=false
# Still waiting: no report file yet.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message?flush=false
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "D2",
  "trade_type": "PI",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "BMOCM01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP2",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### D3 third row, flush=false
# The batch of 3 is full: the report file is created with a header and 3 rows.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message?flush=false
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "D3",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP1",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### D4 fourth row, flush=false
# Waits in a new batch: the file still has 3 rows.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message?flush=false
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "D4",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP1",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}

### D5 fifth row, default flush=true
# flush=true (the default) writes everything waiting: the file now has 5 rows.
POST {{host}}/api/test-message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "trade_ref": "D5",
  "trade_type": "PO",
  "bmoLegalEntity": "LDNBB01",
  "counterpartyId": "CP1",
  "group_cd": "G1"
}


### T1 process the RECEIVED rows of the trade table
# Needs the Postgres table (samples/db) and the stub. Reads the RECEIVED rows in id order, maps each and writes the CSV.
# With the sample rows: read 5, written 3, failed 2 (the failures are in the application log). Add ?limit=2 to stop early.
POST {{host}}/api/trades/process

### T2 process at most 2 rows
POST {{host}}/api/trades/process?limit=2
